INT. MILLINERY SHOP - OAK STREET - 1924 - AFTERNOON

A narrow shop of cut velvet and hatboxes stacked to the ceiling. Dusty sun through the front window. A bell hangs over the door, its clapper tied with ribbon.

MRS. EUGENIA PRATT, 50, proprietress, stands behind the counter in a black dress with a cameo at the throat. She is severe and small and perfectly straight. Beside her, her younger sister CORDELIA, 40, soft-faced, clutches a pincushion like a rosary.

At the door, a boy in a delivery cap, ALOYSIUS, 12, stands with an envelope. He keeps one foot on the sill.

ALOYSIUS
Telegram for Mrs. Pratt.

EUGENIA
Set it down.

CORDELIA
Set it down.

They say it together, then glance at each other.

EUGENIA
Set it down.
^CORDELIA
Don't open it.

Aloysius sets it down on the counter and retreats a step. He watches the sisters as if they might spring. Eugenia does not touch the envelope. She turns her cameo a quarter turn, very slowly. Cordelia takes a pin from the cushion and puts it back.

ALOYSIUS
Is there a reply?

EUGENIA
No.

She slides a coin across the wood. He takes it and leaves, the ribbon-tied bell never ringing. Cordelia lays a hand on the envelope. Eugenia slides it out from under.

Neither looks at it. Both look at the window.
